## 1. Problem inherited from CW-002B

CW-002B gave a history-uniform future-information horizon under the strong assumption

\[
T_{ij}>0
\]

at every one-step transition.

That assumption is unrealistic for many finite-state systems.

A sparse transition graph may forbid many one-step moves while still becoming fully mixing over several steps.

The correct 20th-century interaction is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Perron–Frobenius/Wielandt primitivity}
\times
\text{Birkhoff projective contraction}
\times
\text{nonlinear filtering}
\times
\text{Le Cam deficiency}.
}
\]

The resulting theorem removes one-step positivity.

