# 4. Exact realized-history block contraction

Split a realized history into full blocks of length \(L=\gamma(T)\):

\[
A_1(h),A_2(h),\ldots,A_q(h),
\qquad
q=\left\lfloor\frac nL\right\rfloor.
\]

Each \(A_b(h)\) is strictly positive.

Let

\[
\Delta_b(h)
=
\Delta(A_b(h)),
\]

\[
\tau_b(h)
=
\tanh\left(\frac{\Delta_b(h)}4\right).
\]

The first positive block sends point-mass initial states into the interior of the probability cone.

Therefore

\[
d_H(
\pi_L^{x,h},
\pi_L^{x',h}
)
\le
\Delta_1(h).
\]

Each later positive block gives Birkhoff contraction:

\[
d_H(
\pi_{bL}^{x,h},
\pi_{bL}^{x',h}
)
\le
\tau_b(h)
d_H(
\pi_{(b-1)L}^{x,h},
\pi_{(b-1)L}^{x',h}
).
\]

Hence:

### Theorem CW-002C.1 — Realized block-product forgetting

For every realized positive-likelihood observation history and \(q\ge1\),

\[
\boxed{
d_H(
\pi_{qL}^{x,h},
\pi_{qL}^{x',h}
)
\le
\Delta_1(h)
\prod_{b=2}^{q}\tau_b(h).
}
\]

If \(n=qL+r\) with \(0\le r<L\), the remaining positive linear filter updates are Hilbert-nonexpansive on the cone interior, so the same upper bound remains valid at time \(n\).

Thus define

\[
\boxed{
H_n(h)
=
\Delta_1(h)
\prod_{b=2}^{\lfloor n/L\rfloor}
\tau_b(h).
}
\]

Then

\[
d_H(
\pi_n^{x,h},
\pi_n^{x',h}
)
\le
H_n(h).
\]

This is a data-dependent pathwise certificate.

No global likelihood-ratio bound is needed to compute \(H_n(h)\) from a realized history.

