# 9. Numerical destructive check

A sparse primitive transition matrix was tested:

\[
T=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&1&0\\
0&0&1\\
1/2&1/2&0
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

It is not one-step positive.

Its primitive exponent is

\[
\boxed{L=5}.
\]

Indeed

\[
T^5
=
\begin{pmatrix}
1/4&1/4&1/2\\
1/4&1/2&1/4\\
1/8&3/8&1/2
\end{pmatrix}
>0.
\]

With observation dynamic range bounded by

\[
R=3,
\]

1500 random likelihood histories were generated for each block count.

No violation occurred for the uniform Hilbert or TV bounds.

The uniform cross-ratio bound was deliberately loose, but it remained valid despite the structural zeros in \(T\).

This confirms the theorem's role: a certificate rather than an estimate of typical contraction speed.

