# 10. Literature collision and historical lineage

## Perron / Frobenius

Nonnegative irreducible/primitive matrix structure is classical.

## Wielandt 1950

Wielandt introduced the sharp primitive-exponent bound

\[
\gamma(T)\le(m-1)^2+1.
\]

His original 1950 paper announced the bound; Hans Schneider later published the proof found in Wielandt's notebooks.

## Seneta

Nonnegative matrices, inhomogeneous products, stochastic matrices, and coefficients of ergodicity are developed systematically.

## Atar–Zeitouni / Budhiraja–Ocone / Le Gland–Oudjane

Hilbert/projective contraction and mixing-kernel techniques for nonlinear-filter stability are established.

Le Gland–Oudjane explicitly formulate "mixing kernels" using two-sided domination by a reference measure and derive products of contraction factors.

## Kaijser 1975 / Kochman–Reeds

The finite-HMM literature already handles structural zeros through products of stepping matrices.

Kaijser's Condition A requires the existence of a nonzero subrectangular product; later work uses rank-one closure conditions.

This is more general than primitive-transition + positive-likelihood blocks.

### Autocorrection

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{primitive-block filtering is not the maximal zero-pattern theorem}.
}
\]

It is a clean exact intermediate regime.

